The way to use this is very simple and kinda annoying.
Step 1.) Turn on the Pi, if it's plugged in and not on, unplug it and plug it back in
Step 2.) You will need a monitor, keyboard, and mouse to start up the program, the mouse might be optional but I wouldn't push it
Step 3.) Go to the terminal and type "sudo -E python3 -u ~/QR_reader/code.py", now you can unplug the monitor, keyboard, and mouse
Step 4.) Now scan the badges and sign people in
Step 5.) To end the script plug in the keyboard and press "q"
Step 6.) Now you can just unplug the pi, or plug in monitor and mouse to type "sudo shutdown now" before unplugging

One-time setup: the Google key file
The program looks for the key at ~/QR_reader/service_account.json. Google names the file something like "fifth-chalice-487115-u5-1f3b8eea7e11.json" when you download it, and the old version of code.py used that name, so it has to be renamed once.
Before Step 3, the first time you run the updated code (or after putting a new key on the Pi), go to the terminal and type "mv ~/QR_reader/fifth-chalice-487115-u5-1f3b8eea7e11.json ~/QR_reader/service_account.json" (use the real file name if it's different)
To check it worked, type "ls ~/QR_reader" and look for service_account.json
If you skip this, the program stops right away with "FileNotFoundError" and nothing lights up
Never copy the key into the GitHub repo, it works like a password for the attendance sheet
